					<span class="printuser deleted" data-id="1361261"><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/common--images/avatars/default/a16.png" alt=""/>(account deleted)</span> <span class="odate time_1386180467 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">04 Dec 2013 18:07</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1908139">
									

<p>Humanity's curiosity and drive towards technology and innovation is the result of a skip? No. Absolutely not.</p>
<p>I generally don't like articles that attribute anomalous causes to phenomena that have perfectly reasonable scientific explanations. And this is that problem writ large.</p>
<p>Also, I'm really not liking the reliance on author meta-commentary for this. If your intent was to create a puzzle and lurk the thread to congratulate people on &quot;solving&quot; it, then I guess you've achieved what you're going for. As an act of storytelling, though, this feels like a bundle of gimmicks (the memetic stuff, temporal weirdness) obscuring a main idea that frankly is kind of ridiculous. Meta-fuckery is tricky; it can be used to expand the story of an article in new and interesting ways, or it can be used to cover up something that would be otherwise unworkable. In this case I feel it's the latter.</p>

							</div>

								
					
